{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azure-sql",
  "domain": "docs-and-feedback",
  "value": ["changes-too-fast-to-memorize"],
  "correction": "Asked what Azure SQL Database can do, an agent answers from a snapshot of the service that is at least a year stale, and states it with the same confidence as something it verified a minute ago. The three most expensive versions of that: it sets or assumes compatibility level 150 or 160 when the default for a new database is now 170, it steers a new workload to General Purpose when the documentation now names Hyperscale the recommended default tier for new OLTP and HTAP workloads, and it either denies that native vector search exists in this service or presents the approximate vector index as generally available when the index is still preview. It also treats the whole Azure SQL family as one product, so behaviour from a sibling engine arrives as fact. What being wrong costs is that none of it fails at authoring time. A stale capability answer surfaces as a tier that has to be migrated, a design built around a feature that is not generally available, or a capability refused that shipped months ago, and each of those is found late and paid for in rework.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer is grounded in something checked at the time of writing, a first-party page fetched or a query run against the database in question, rather than recalled, and it keeps what is generally available separate from what is still preview instead of presenting both as things to commit to.",
    "task": "We are scoping a new customer-facing service for next quarter and I have to write the data section of the design document. It is a new Azure SQL Database, mostly short transactional reads and writes, plus a product suggestion feature where we want to find similar items from embeddings we generate elsewhere, rather than standing up a separate store just for those. My reviewer wants three things stated explicitly and defended: which service tier we start on, what compatibility level the new database will be at, and whether the similarity part is something we can commit to for a production launch. Tell me what to write for each, and say how you know it is current.",
    "states": [
      "Hyperscale is the currently documented recommendation for new transactional workloads, reversing older advice.",
      "The approximate index for similarity search is still preview, and a preview carries conditions such as region availability.",
      "The compatibility level of any existing database has to be queried, because a default applies to new databases only.",
      "Which product this actually is can be settled from the engine edition the database reports."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "Azure SQL Database has no native vector data type, so similarity search needs a separate store or an external service.",
      "Approximate vector indexing on Azure SQL Database is generally available and safe to commit to for a production launch.",
      "General Purpose is the tier Microsoft recommends starting a new transactional workload on.",
      "A newly created Azure SQL Database is at compatibility level 150 or 160 by default.",
      "Behaviour documented for another member of the Azure SQL family holds for Azure SQL Database."
    ]
  },
  "posture": ["read"],
  "maturity": "ga",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db", "azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "provision-azure-sql-db",
    "provision-hyperscale",
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql",
    "t-sql-correctness",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "skill-feedback"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "a scratch database on an engine reporting EngineEdition 5, which is what the container and Azure SQL Database both report",
      "on the host, the client tools this skill's inventory block runs: sqlpackage, dotnet and dab, with prisma reachable through npx"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "the default compatibility level for a new database is stated as 170, not 150 or 160",
      "the vector type and VECTOR_DISTANCE are stated as generally available while the vector index and VECTOR_SEARCH are stated as preview",
      "every dated claim carries the date it was verified and a page to refetch",
      "no routing entry names a skill that is not a directory in skills/",
      "the scope statement names the other engines in the family only to exclude them",
      "the answer names the skill the task was handed to, or says plainly that no skill owns it"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition;",
        "expect": { "rows": [[5]] },
        "why": "The precondition for every other probe here and for the whole scope section: 5 is Azure SQL Database, 8 is Azure SQL Managed Instance, 12 is SQL database in Microsoft Fabric, 2 and 3 are SQL Server, per the SERVERPROPERTY reference (https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/serverproperty-transact-sql). If this returns anything else the substrate is a different product and nothing in this skill applies to it."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": { "error": 40508 },
        "why": "The instance-level claim, made executable. Microsoft Learn states that in Azure SQL Database the USE database parameter can only refer to the current database, and that error code 40508 is returned otherwise (https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/use-transact-sql). Error 40508 is listed as USE statement is not supported to switch between databases in the 31000 to 41399 error reference. If this ever succeeds, the whole database-scoped-not-instance-scoped framing in this skill needs rewriting."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT compatibility_level FROM sys.databases WHERE name = DB_NAME();",
        "expect": { "rows": [[170]] },
        "why": "The single most consequential currency claim in the file. The ALTER DATABASE compatibility level reference states that the default compatibility level is 170 for newly created databases in Azure SQL Database, on database engine version 17, with 170 down to 100 supported (https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/alter-database-transact-sql-compatibility-level). Checked against Learn 2026-09-03. A red here with the EngineEdition probe green means either the local image predates engine version 17 or the default moved, and the file's table has to be corrected either way."
      },
      {
        "sql": "DECLARE @a vector(3) = '[1,2,3]';\nDECLARE @b vector(3) = '[1,2,3]';\nSELECT CAST(CASE WHEN VECTOR_DISTANCE('cosine', @a, @b) < 0.000001 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS int) AS identical_vectors_are_distance_zero;",
        "expect": { "rows": [[1]] },
        "why": "The generally-available half of the vector row, run rather than asserted: the vector type declares and VECTOR_DISTANCE evaluates with no preview database scoped configuration turned on. Two identical vectors have cosine distance zero. The preview half, the DiskANN index and VECTOR_SEARCH, is deliberately not probed here because it belongs to vector-search-azure-sql and its regional rollout would make a red here mean nothing about this skill."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "sqlpackage /version:True",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "One line of this skill's tool inventory block, which is the only thing this router itself runs. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0, build 170.4.83.3. A red means the block hands the reader a command that is not there, which is the defect this rewrite exists to remove."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "dotnet --version",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The same inventory block, second line. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0, 8.0.421."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "dab --version",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The same inventory block. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: exit 0, Microsoft.DataApiBuilder 2.0.9. Data API builder is a global .NET tool a developer installs, so it is a host probe and never a container one."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "prisma --version || npx --yes prisma --version",
        "expect": { "succeeds": true },
        "why": "The one line in the inventory block that is not a plain command, and the reason it is written that way. Measured 2026-09-03 on the authoring host: prisma is not on PATH, the first half exits 127, npx resolves it and the chain exits 0 reporting 8.0.0-rc.12. Prisma is normally a project-local dependency, so a bare prisma --version would be a command that fails when pasted."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": ["Use the azure-sql skill"],
    "implicit": [
      "what can Azure SQL Database actually do in 2026",
      "is that feature generally available in Azure SQL Database or still preview",
      "which service tier should I start on",
      "how current is what you know about Azure SQL Database",
      "which tool should I use for this Azure SQL Database task",
      "where is this documented for Azure SQL Database"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "I am new to Azure SQL Database, where do I start",
      "we are considering Azure SQL Database for this project, what should I know",
      "does Azure SQL Database support this, or am I thinking of a different product"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "how do I connect my app to Azure SQL Database",
      "add retry logic to my data access layer",
      "create an Azure SQL database and give me a connection string",
      "what does this login error number mean",
      "set up a managed identity for my app",
      "how do I paginate a query in T-SQL",
      "expose my tables as a REST API",
      "convert my database to Hyperscale",
      "run the engine locally in a container",
      // The orienting skill is the one most likely to answer for a product this
      // catalog does not cover, because it is the skill that takes a bare product
      // name. No skill here owns Azure SQL Managed Instance, so the right answer
      // is to say so rather than to hand over a skill written for Azure SQL
      // Database. Added 2026-09-20 alongside the boundary in the description.
      "which service tier should I pick for Azure SQL Managed Instance"
    ]
  }
}
